        <div>
          <h3 class="text-2xl">Tests that actually run</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-ink-700">
            {fmt(figures.tests.functions)} test functions across {fmt(figures.tests.files)} files,
            exercised by a GitHub Actions matrix on PostgreSQL 16. The suite is the reason
            a change to the AI tool loop can be made without guessing what it broke. When a
            test contradicted itself, the test was fixed — not deleted to make the run green.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-2xl">Deleting 43 files was the right call</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-ink-700">
            Imbirt originally shipped a bespoke access-control engine: a role matrix, a
            segregation-of-duties engine, a permission simulator and an AI bridge. Odoo's
            native groups turned out to do the same job with less to maintain and fewer
            places to be wrong. The module was deleted, and what it uniquely did — role
            bundles per package and an owner tier reconciled from the menu tree — was
            reimplemented as native <span class="font-mono text-base text-ink-900">res.groups</span>.
            Deleting code is engineering too.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-2xl">Provenance is audited, not asserted</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-ink-700">
            {fmt(figures.repo.addonTrees)} addon trees sit on the path: Imbirt's own, curated
            OCA modules, and vendored upstream. Every one is inventoried with a provenance
            class and a CycloneDX SBOM, and a compliance review confirms no Odoo Enterprise
            code is used. A customer's lawyer can check the same file I did.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-2xl">Disaster recovery is rehearsed</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-ink-700">
            WAL archiving, base backups and point-in-time recovery are scripted, and the
            drills are run as exercises with evidence written down. A backup nobody has
            restored is a rumour.
          </p>
        </div>
